Date: Thu, 18 Nov 1993 17:37:47 -0500 (EST)
From: david@bitdance.MV.COM (R. David Murray)
Subject: Oceania vs. Cyberspatial Libertaria

I agree with Nick that Oceana is not likely to succeed as currently
envisioned.  And I wish them all the luck in the world.

Neil Schulman, in Alongside Night, postulates a purpose-built underground
arcology for unregulated trading, supposing that the money saved by the
customers would pay for the construction and operation.  He might be right,
but again the problem is that the up front costs are too high, absent a
demonstrated demand.  In other words, things aren't bad enough yet.

If things get that bad . . . you look at the former SU, and see a place
where things got that bad.  Yet purpose built agoras did not spring
up.  That might mean the up-front capital cost is just too high.  Or it
might mean that there was not enough of a tradition of freedom,
entrepreneurship, and economic awareness in the subject population
to provide the philosophical foundation for a successful effort.

I suspect it is a combination.  I suspect if things get that bad here
(and Clinton is trying hard with his UnHealth Pogrom), there will be
enough desire and know-how to build something.

And I think Nick is right about what it will look like.

The capital costs for his proposal are much smaller.  Such a TAZ could
start as a quasi-legal operation, and maybe even stay quasi-legal.

One thing that Nick fails to mention, though.  The economy is undergoing
a transformation from material-centered (manufacturing) to
information-centered.  Or rather, is completing that transformation.
As Tim points out, the jobs being lost in the old sectors are not coming
back.

But this means that information-centric jobs will come to have the
overwhelming majority of the value of the economy.  Sure, we will still
be physical (at least until we upload), but the disproportionate amount
of value we will hold and manipulate will be in the form of information.

And we all know what that implies.

Sure, we'll still need money to eat.  But suppose that in tomorrow's world
80% of the value that you wish to hold is in the form of information,
which you can buy in cypherspace.  That means that only 20% of your
time is spent earning money to pay for the physical value you hold.

So why not have a part time, 'legitimate', job that pays for the physical?
The other 80% of your time, you can spend in cypherspace.

And Nick's TAZ's will give you a place to satisfy other physical
desires using some of the cash generated by your cypherspace
activities.

By the time these trends play themselves out, the State will be no more ...

This is not just theory, by the way.  I have anecdotal evidence to back it
up <grin>.

I'm currently intentionally working only a part time job.  The physical
needs of my wife and I are more than met by the combination of her salary
(doing a job she enjoys, BTW) and my part time pay. A (very) few of the
activities I am engaged in during my remaining time do not now and
probably never will produce cash income.  But they do allow me to
barter for information services I find valuable.  I expect this to
become more true as time goes along.  Especially when it is no longer
direct barter, but a cypherspace cash economy.

Although it is possible I will return to full time employment in the
future, if it happens it will almost certainly be for reasons of job
satisfaction, not out of necessity.

So the trend is already reality.

-- 
