Date: Thu, 18 Nov 1993 18:38:33 -0500 (EST)
From: david@bitdance.MV.COM (R. David Murray)
Subject: Getting paid for Net surfing

Tim, I'd like to hear more about this getting paid for net surfing, if
you've got any more info.  If I could earn $100/hr net surfing, that
would sure beat my current part time job.

I can certainly imagine it working like this (and I'm going to get to
try it in practice, I think): good computer consultants can easily make
$100/hr.  It is much easier to be a good computer consultant if you
know how to surf the net and can therefore use it as a *primo* source
of help for solving client problems.  In fact, for me it is an
indispensable resource.

But if there are companies that understand the Net just enough to be asking
directly for Net surfers, I'd love to get in touch with them . . . 

In fact, though, this is the perfect entre to an idea I've been meaning to
bring up.  I thought briefly about keeping this under my hat and trying
to implement it as a business, but the discussions about the futility of
people without business experience doing that kept the thought pretty brief.
Besides, on further reflection I think it works best using the traditional
(on the Internet) decentralized model.

I think the Net is ready for a service I call the AnswerMan service.
It's kind of like what AMIX tried to do, but not quite.  Or maybe what
AMIX would evolve into once it had a Net connection.  (Anyone heard any
good rumors lately, by the way?  I doubt I'll log in to it again until
they either get a Net connection or I hear it is a happening place).

AnswerMan would be a clearing house.  Kind of like the comp. groups on
netnews, but instead of intellectual barter, it would operate on a cash
basis.  I envision it operating like this:

You have a question or a problem.  You post a message to an appropriate
set of topics.  People with knowledge in those topic areas see your note.
They put together a proposal for solving your problem (the solution could be
simply an answer to your question, or an offer to sell you a program,
or an offer to write a program on contract, or just about anything).
The proposal has an attached price.  The customer chooses the combination
of price, proposal, and proposer-reputation that he likes best, and gets his
problem solved.

Clearly there are lots of implementation details here.  Is there one
best method for taking bids, or does the customer specify a bid
protocol with his question-post?  Are prices open to negotiation after
acceptance? Etc., etc.  Lots of this stuff can be answered by looking
at current business practice and translating it into software.  Or at
least writing software to enable it to happen electronically.

The other absolutely necessary things would be reputation data bases and
an arbitration mechanism.  The structure of both of these are non-trivial
(but definitely solvable) problems.

So, anyone interested in discussing how to implement this?  It seems to
me it ought to be possible to do it with very little capital outlay,
as a cooperative Net project.  We'd all benefit from the results, since
we're all experts in something, right?  Maybe we could start a new
netnews hierarchy and figure out how to improve the netnews software to
facilitate this.  No one would even have to take any financial 
responsibility, since payment could be completely a transaction between
the interested parties.  That would mean small amount transactions
would probably not occur; too much overhead.  But the system would still
be useful, and somebody would step forward to broker small amounts and
make them profitable.  For example, one could do small transactions in
Thornes, and Thorne brokers could deal with money-changing accumulated
totals into dollars.

The best thing is, this would be the perfect test bed for positive
reputations and competition in reputation/arbitration services.  And we'd
want to build cryptography into it from the start.

Oh, about that name.  If we want to bring the Net public in as users of
this, we'd better come up with a better one.  AnswerMan has a much better
cadence than, say, AnswerPerson or Answers Unlimited, but it is too
Politically Incorrect to be marketable.

-- 
david                                     (BitDance Enterprises)

PS: IMO, AMIX made the mistake of adopting the traditional information-as-
property model, and set up a market to sell it.  Instead, we are moving into
an era of information-as-service.  To sell information successfully, we
must set up a market that sells *service*, not information per-se.

Heh.  It occurs to me that that statement pretty much (finally) resolves
for me my position on information-as-property.  I don't believe in it.
After I've thought about this more, maybe I'll write an essay on the idea.

