From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Oceania vs. Cyberspatial Libertaria

R. David Murray:
> The economy is undergoing
> a transformation from material-centered (manufacturing) to
> information-centered.  

This is overrated.  The vast majority of our needs are still
physical (food, housing, medicine, etc.), and while many of
the jobs themselves involve handling information, the 
physical plant is still there as a vulnerable bottlneck between
the info economy and meeting our needs.

> So why not have a part time, 'legitimate', job that pays for the physical?
> The other 80% of your time, you can spend in cypherspace.

More likely, X% of people  will have jobs in cyberspace,
and Y% in the physical world, but all will get paid in
cyberspace.  After all money is just information, and it won't be 
that hard to transfer between various forms of it.  Money 
translation will be one of the bottlenecks during start-up phase, 
though.

