From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: Drugs: questions/discussion

> From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
> > Can one really buy LSD that cheap? [$3]
> 
> Yes, unless the stepped-up efforts of law enforcement have changed
> things drastically recently.

I handled a network drug-price survey for some time before handing
it off for the summer.  Thus I have some reasonably good price data
on this: LSD in end-use quantities typically costs from $1 to $5 per
tab, with the lower prices usually being on the West Coast,
specifically California.  However, one tab typically represents
about 75 micrograms of the drug, while some people may use 300 mcg
and up in order to get the full spectrum of effects.  Prices in
other countries may be as much as 30 to 50 $US.  

> Hallucinogens work (partly at least) by temporarily suppressing
> serotonin to a profound extent, eliminating its inhibitory effects.

This was an attractive theory, but it hasn't held up.  For one
thing, potent serotonin antagonists (blockers) need not be
psychoactive at all.  The "classical psychedelics" are mixed
agonist/antagonists of serotonin, meaning that they promote activity
at certain subclasses of serotonin receptors while blocking it at
others.  The key activity appears to be agonism -- promotion, rather
than suppression -- at the 1c and 2 populations, but there is plenty
of evidence that we really don't understand what's going on.

> > Does PIHKAL stand for anything?
> Psychoactives (or maybe Psychedelics) I Have Known And Loved.

"Phenethylamines", to nit-pick.  This means it only(!) covers
compounds like mescaline, STP, and MDMA, rather than indole
derivatives like DMT or LSD.  I'll third the recommendation of
this book -- I need to go harass the guy who `borrowed' my copy.

