From: smo@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Shawn O'Connor)
Subject: Still...

> Timothy C. May says:
> > Perry Metzger writes:
> > 
> > > likely to be doing a whole lot better in lifestyle. If coercion is to
> > > be used to impose anarchy, of course, it isn't anarchy any more.
> > 
> > Just so there's no confusion, not all of us anarchists are against the
> > use of force. If it is "coercive" to use force to dismantle the police
> > force I don't want, the health care system I don't want (but am
> > commanded to pay for anyway), and so on, then I have no philosophical
> > problem with using "coercion."
> 
> "Coercion" is a term of art meaning "the initiation of force against
> persons or their property, or the credible threat of such initiation".
> It does not refer to things like self defense, kicking people off your
> land, etc.
> 
> Perry

The end of governments will not be the end of coercion.  The end of 
the statist oligopoly on the use of force will be marked by an 
increase of private interests resorting to force to attain their
goals.  For example, private interests building a highway may 
have problems with a few holdout land-owners demanding absurd sums
for their property.  If it is indeed, overall, less expensive to 
simply take the land, it's likely to happen.  Even if the highwaymen 
recompense those affected, it's not Libertaria.  Although I think the
Non-Coercion Principle is a good rule of thumb,  in discussions of PPL
it's likely to mislead the uninitiated.  Better that they read the 
arguments for why an anarchic society will be more peaceful and
give it some thought.

